  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1203 PM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nA notable downtrend in convection is then forecast for Tuesday and\nWednesday as the upper high builds more directly on top of NM.\nEnsemble mean heights look to rise to around 594-595 decameters\nwith a few deterministic members showing a couple decameters\nhigher. Temperatures will rise and offer instability, but the \nstrong high will entrain drier air and cut off any significant \nmonsoon moisture plumes while introducing a bit of subsidence. \nStorms will still initiate over the high terrain, but will become \nless efficient at rainfall production with smaller footprints \nreaching the ground due to lower PWATs of generally (0.7 to 0.9 \ninch). \n\nDeterministic models are indicating that a central plains \nshortwave will send a surface boundary into southeastern CO and \nfar northeastern NM which may act as a brief catalyst for storms \nthere late Wednesday. This boundary would only offer a few degrees\nof cooling over eastern plains zones with all areas still staying\nabove normal into Thursday. The hope is that this backdoor front \nwould advect increasing low layer moisture farther south and west \nfor more widespread shower and thunderstorm development Thursday \nand Friday afternoons, but the PWATs only look to rise back to a \n0.8 to 1.0 inch range which may only bring a modest increase in \nstorm coverage. The upper high will orbit close to NM through \nnext weekend too, keeping us dependent on recycled moisture. The \nabove normal temperatures will creep within a couple to a few \ndegrees of Heat Advisory thresholds in the upper 90's and low \n100's Tuesday onward.",
